## WP3 — Making notifications actually correct

Six defects, all found by reading the current code. Fix all six.

### 3.1 Read markers never persisted

Done in WP1.3. Without it nothing else in this work package matters.

### 3.2 Room messages never refresh the badge

`src/routes/api/send/+server.ts`: the DM branch computes `const unread = await total_unread(env, to)`
and puts it in the push payload; the group branch does not. So a room message leaves the badge
stale.

Fix — in the group branch, compute per-recipient unread and send one push per recipient. `notify()`
takes a uid array and one shared payload, so a per-recipient count needs one `notify()` call per
uid. A room can be large; bound the work:

```ts
// One push per recipient so each carries that person's own badge count. Bounded because
// `targets` is already only the members who were offline for this message.
await Promise.all(
    targets.map(async (uid) => {
        const unread = await total_unread(env, uid, [group_conv_id(group)]);
        return push(env, [uid], { …payload, unread });
    })
);
```

If you would rather keep a single `notify()` call, the acceptable fallback is to omit `unread` for
rooms and have the service worker call `sync_badge()` instead — but then implement §3.3's fallback
branch. Prefer the per-recipient version.

### 3.3 The service worker never updates the app badge on push

`src/service-worker.ts` push handler shows the notification and stops. `payload.unread` is carried
all the way from the API and then dropped.

In the `push` listener, after `showNotification`:

```ts
if (typeof (data as { unread?: number } | null)?.unread === 'number') {
    await set_badge((data as { unread: number }).unread);
}
```

`set_badge` lives in `src/lib/badge.ts` and is already error-guarded. Import it — it has no DOM
dependencies, so it is safe inside the worker.

Also set the badge on the `mark-read` action (§3.5) — `POST /api/read` already returns the fresh
total.

### 3.4 The notification reply action corrupts room replies

`src/service-worker.ts:126-138`:

```ts
body: JSON.stringify({ to: data.conv.split('|').find((u) => u !== data.id), text: reply })
```

For a room the conv is `` `g:<gid>` `` — no `|`, so `split('|')` yields `['g:<gid>']` and `.find()`
returns that whole string. The reply is then POSTed as `{ to: 'g:abc' }`, a DM to a user id that does
not exist. The reply is silently lost.

Worse, `data.id` is the *message* id (`src/routes/api/send/+server.ts:105,128` set `id: m.id`), not
the sender's uid — so even the DM case's `.find(u => u !== data.id)` never excludes anything and
happens to return the first uid of the sorted pair, which is the recipient (you) half the time.
**Both halves of this are broken.**

Fix by carrying an explicit reply target in the payload rather than reverse-engineering it. In
`src/routes/api/send/+server.ts`:

- group branch payload: add `kind: 'r'`, `reply_to: group`
- DM branch payload: add `kind: 'u'`, `reply_to: me.id`

Then in the service worker:

```ts
if (event.action === 'reply' && data?.reply_to) {
    const reply = (event as NotificationEvent & { reply?: string }).reply;
    if (reply) {
        event.waitUntil(
            fetch('/api/send', {
                method: 'POST',
                headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
                body: JSON.stringify(
                    data.kind === 'r'
                        ? { group: data.reply_to, text: reply }
                        : { to: data.reply_to, text: reply }
                )
            }).catch(() => {})
        );
    }
    return;
}
```

Extend `NotifyPayload` in `src/lib/sw-core.ts` with `kind?: 'u' | 'r'` and `reply_to?: string`, and
have `notification_from` copy both into `options.data`. Update the `data` type in the
`notificationclick` handler.

### 3.5 `mark-read` leaves the notification on screen and the badge stale

Current handler fires the fetch and returns without closing the notification. Fix:

```ts
if (event.action === 'mark-read' && data?.conv) {
    event.notification.close();
    event.waitUntil(
        fetch('/api/read', {
            method: 'POST',
            headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
            body: JSON.stringify({ conv: data.conv })
        })
            .then((r) => (r.ok ? r.json() : null))
            .then((b) => (b ? set_badge(b.total as number) : undefined))
            .catch(() => {})
    );
    return;
}
```

### 3.6 A backgrounded tab counts as "delivered", so no push arrives

This is the single biggest cause of "I don't get notifications". `relay` reports `delivered: true`
whenever *any* socket for that uid accepted the frame (`ws/src/hub.ts:132-149`). A phone with the
PWA open but backgrounded still holds an open socket. The API therefore skips the push, and the user
sees nothing until they reopen the app.

Fix: track per-socket foreground state and only count *active* sockets as delivered. Messages still
go to every socket — only the delivery *verdict* changes.

**Client — `src/lib/ws.ts`:**

```ts
// The server uses this to decide whether a live socket counts as "the user saw it".
// A backgrounded PWA keeps its socket open, so without this every push is suppressed.
function report_active(): void {
    if (typeof document === 'undefined') return;
    const on = document.visibilityState === 'visible';
    // `keep` so the state is re-sent on every reconnect — a socket that reconnects while
    // hidden must not come back marked active.
    ws_send({ type: 'active', on }, true);
}
```

Call `report_active()` at the end of `s.onopen`, and register
`document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', report_active)` once at module scope (guard for SSR
with `typeof document !== 'undefined'`). Because `keep` messages are de-duplicated by exact JSON
string (`ws_send` line 112), the `{type:'active',on:true}` and `{type:'active',on:false}` variants
would both accumulate in the handshake queue. Use `ws_drop` to remove the opposite variant before
sending:

```ts
ws_drop({ type: 'active', on: !on });
ws_send({ type: 'active', on }, true);
```

**Server — `ws/src/hub.ts`:**

In `webSocketMessage`, add a branch:

```ts
} else if (msg.type === 'active') {
    // serializeAttachment survives DO hibernation; a plain field would not
    ws.serializeAttachment({ active: msg.on === true });
}
```

Change `deliver()` to report only active sockets, while still sending to all:

```ts
private deliver(uid: string, payload: unknown): boolean {
    const data = JSON.stringify(payload);
    const sockets = this.state.getWebSockets(uid);
    let seen = false;
    for (const ws of sockets) {
        try {
            ws.send(data);
            // A socket that never reported its state is assumed active — old clients
            // must keep working, and over-reporting delivery is the pre-existing
            // behaviour, not a regression.
            const att = ws.deserializeAttachment() as { active?: boolean } | null;
            if (att?.active !== false) seen = true;
        } catch {
            try { ws.close(1011, 'delivery failed'); } catch {}
        }
    }
    return seen;
}
```

The "assume active when unreported" default is deliberate: it degrades to today's behaviour rather
than to a push storm.

**Interaction with `should_notify`** (`src/lib/sw-core.ts:119`): when the tab *is* foregrounded and
on the target conversation, no push is sent by the server at all now; if one arrives anyway (race),
`should_notify` still suppresses the visible toast. The two layers are complementary — keep both.

### 3.7 Presence checks must not count backgrounded sockets either

`ChatHub` `/check` (`ws/src/hub.ts:53-56`) returns `getWebSockets().length > 0`. Reuse the same
attachment so "online now" (WP6) means "actually looking at their phone":

```ts
if (url.pathname === '/check') {
    const online = this.state.getWebSockets().some((ws) => {
        const att = ws.deserializeAttachment() as { active?: boolean } | null;
        return att?.active !== false;
    });
    return new Response(JSON.stringify({ online }));
}
```

